---
title: Render targets
description: Declare named off-screen surfaces for post-processing passes.
---

Named render targets provide explicit intermediate surfaces in the post-scene render graph. They are distinct from storage textures and the private A/B textures owned by `PingPongShaderPass`.

## Declare targets

```svelte
<FragCanvas
	{material}
	renderTargets={{
		half: { scale: 0.5 },
		fixed: { width: 512, height: 512, format: 'rgba16float' }
	}}
	{passes}
/>
```

Use each key as a pass `input` or `output`. Named targets must be written before they are read in the current frame, and named routing requires `needsSwap: false`.

## Definition

| Field | Default | Resolution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `width` | — | Explicit width, floored to an integer |
| `height` | — | Explicit height, floored to an integer |
| `scale` | `1` | Applied independently to omitted dimensions |
| `format` | Current working format | GPU texture format |

Every supplied dimension and scale must be finite and greater than zero. Resolved dimensions have a minimum of one pixel. Keys follow the same WGSL-safe identifier rule as other named resources.

If only one explicit dimension is supplied, the other still follows the canvas and `scale`. For a 1920×1080 canvas, `{ width: 512 }` resolves to 512×1080 with the default scale.

## Format and lifecycle

In the standard SDR path, the default target format follows the preferred canvas format. HDR, tone-mapped, or explicitly configured working pipelines can use `rgba16float`. A target-level `format` always wins.

Spektral validates the working format and named targets against the active device before allocation or pipeline creation. Built-in passes require float-sampled inputs and float-renderable outputs because their shaders use `texture_2d<f32>` and `vec4f`. Integer and depth targets remain available to a custom `RenderPass` with a matching typed pipeline, but routing one through a built-in pass produces an early Spektral diagnostic. Float32 working formats use non-filtering sampling and nearest presentation unless the device exposes `float32-filterable`.

The renderer compares resolved targets by `key:format:widthxheight`. It allocates new targets, reallocates changed targets, destroys removed targets, and preserves textures whose signature remains stable.

Custom render passes receive the resolved map as `context.targets`. Built-in passes access named targets through normal slot routing.

For persistent fragment feedback, declare a sampled material texture and use `PingPongShaderPass`. For compute writes, declare a storage texture. See [Render Passes](/docs/render-passes) and [Textures](/docs/textures).
